1
00:00:01,959 --> 00:00:05,559
censured for showing cowardice in the battle.

2
00:00:05,559 --> 00:00:10,200
Once again, some of the animals heard this with a certain bewilderment, but

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:16,319
Squealer was soon able to convince them that their memories had been at fault.

4
00:00:16,319 --> 00:00:21,719
In the autumn, by a tremendous exhausting effort, for the harvest had to be gathered at

5
00:00:21,719 --> 00:00:26,600
almost the same time, the windmill was finished.

6
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:31,639
The machinery had still to be installed, and Whymper was negotiating the purchase of it,

7
00:00:31,639 --> 00:00:34,479
but the structure was completed.

8
00:00:34,479 --> 00:00:39,600
In the teeth of every difficulty, in spite of inexperience, of primitive

9
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:46,680
implements, of bad luck, and of Snowball's treachery, the work had been finished punctually to

10
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:49,000
the very day.

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Tired out, but proud, the animals walked round and round their masterpiece, which

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,479
appeared even more beautiful in their eyes than when it had been built the first time.

13
00:01:00,479 --> 00:01:04,600
Moreover, the walls were twice as thick as before.

14
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:08,559
Nothing short of explosives would lay them low this time.

15
00:01:08,559 --> 00:01:12,120
And when they thought of how they had labored, what discouragements they had

16
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:16,239
overcome, and the enormous difference that would be made in their lives when

17
00:01:16,239 --> 00:01:21,639
the sails were turning and the dynamos running, when they thought of all this,

18
00:01:21,639 --> 00:01:25,559
their tiredness forsook them, and they gamboled round and round the

19
00:01:25,559 --> 00:01:29,399
windmill, uttering cries of triumph.

20
00:01:29,399 --> 00:01:34,520
Napoleon himself, attended by his dogs and his cockerel, came down to inspect

21
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:36,440
the completed work.

22
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:40,000
He personally congratulated the animals on their achievement,

23
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,520
and announced that the mill would be named Napoleon Mill.

24
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:50,600
Two days later, the animals were called together for a special meeting in the barn.

25
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:55,040
They were struck dumb with surprise when Napoleon announced that he had sold the

26
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:58,120
pile of timber to Frederick.

27
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:03,239
Tomorrow, Frederick's wagons would arrive and begin carting it away.

28
00:02:03,239 --> 00:02:07,079
Throughout the whole period of his seeming friendship with Pilkington,

29
00:02:07,079 --> 00:02:11,319
Napoleon had really been in secret agreement with Frederick.

30
00:02:11,319 --> 00:02:14,399
All relations with Foxwood had been broken off.

31
00:02:14,399 --> 00:02:17,760
Insulting messages had been sent to Pilkington.

32
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:21,559
The pigeons had been told to avoid Pinchfield Farm and to alter their

33
00:02:21,559 --> 00:02:27,280
slogan from death to Frederick to death to Pilkington.

34
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:31,559
At the same time, Napoleon assured the animals that the stories of an impending

35
00:02:31,559 --> 00:02:37,000
attack on Animal Farm were completely untrue, and that the tales about Frederick's

36
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,159
cruelty to his own animals had been greatly exaggerated.

37
00:02:42,159 --> 00:02:47,639
All these rumors had probably originated with Snowball and his agents.

38
00:02:47,639 --> 00:02:53,719
It now appeared that Snowball was not, after all, hiding on Pinchfield Farm,

39
00:02:53,719 --> 00:02:57,319
and, in fact, had never been there in his life.

40
00:02:57,319 --> 00:03:03,120
He was living in considerable luxury, so it was said, that Foxwood,

41
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:09,159
and had, in reality, been a pensioner of Pilkington for years past.

42
00:03:09,159 --> 00:03:13,680
The pigs were in ecstasies over Napoleon's cunning.

43
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:21,719
By seeming to be friendly with Pilkington, he had forced Frederick to raise his price by 12 lb.

44
00:03:21,719 --> 00:03:26,319
But the superior quality of Napoleon's mind, said Squealer, was shown in the

45
00:03:26,319 --> 00:03:31,120
fact that he trusted nobody, not even Frederick.

46
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:35,440
Frederick had wanted to pay for the timber with something called a check,

47
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:40,639
which, it seemed, was a piece of paper with a promise to pay written upon it.

48
00:03:40,639 --> 00:03:43,479
But Napoleon was too clever for him.

49
00:03:43,479 --> 00:03:49,239
He had demanded payment in real 5 lb notes, which were to be handed over

50
00:03:49,239 --> 00:03:51,840
before the timber was removed.

51
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:57,040
Already, Frederick had paid up, and the sum he had paid was just enough to buy

52
00:03:57,040 --> 00:04:00,719
the machinery for the windmill.

53
00:04:01,399 --> 00:04:05,440
Meanwhile, the timber was being carted away at high speed.

54
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:09,440
When it was all gone, another special meeting was held in the barn for the

55
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:13,200
animals to inspect Frederick's banknotes.

56
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:18,559
Smiling beatifically, and wearing both his decorations, Napoleon reposed on a

57
00:04:18,559 --> 00:04:23,799
bed of straw on the platform, with the money at his side, neatly piled on a

58
00:04:23,799 --> 00:04:26,000
China dish from the farmhouse kitchen.
